This is the one that matters most. CMS vulnerabilities and outdated JavaScript libraries are real attack vectors, especially for smaller organizations running their own web infrastructure. SSC finds these; we don't.

**How to close it:**

| Tool | What It Adds | Cost |
|------|-------------|------|
| **nuclei** (ProjectDiscovery) | Open-source vulnerability scanner. 7,000+ templates for CVE detection, CMS fingerprinting, misconfigurations, exposed panels, default credentials. This is the single biggest improvement we could make. | **Free** (open source, runs locally) |
| **httpx** (ProjectDiscovery) | Fast HTTP toolkit — technology fingerprinting, status codes, response analysis. Identifies what's running (WordPress, Drupal, nginx version, etc.) | **Free** (open source) |
| **wappalyzer** (via npm) | Technology detection — identifies CMS, frameworks, JavaScript libraries, analytics tools from HTTP responses | **Free** (open source library) |

Installing nuclei alone would close probably 60-70% of the app security gap. It's what SSC is essentially doing at scale — scanning for known vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, and exposed components.

```bash
brew install nuclei httpx
```

That's it. No API cost. We add a `check_web_vulnerabilities()` function to assess.py that runs nuclei against the target domain. The templates are community-maintained and updated constantly.

### Network Security — **Partially worth closing. Diminishing returns.**

The gap here is that SSC scans more IP ranges per organization and has historical scan data. We scan what the domain resolves to + Shodan data for those IPs.

